WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:08.780
Have you ever wondered how it’s possible
to scratch a CD or DVD and still have it play

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.860
back whatever it’s storing?

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.230
The scratch really does affect the 1’s and
0’s on the disc, so it reads off different

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:20.570
data from what was stored, but unless it’s
really scratched up, the bits it reads are

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:26.100
decoded into precisely the same file that
was encoded on to it, a bit-for-bit copy,

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:27.860
despite all those errors.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:32.780
There’s a whole pile of mathematical cleverness
that allows us to store data, and just as

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:37.050
importantly to transmit it, in a way that’s
resilient to errors.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:42.199
Well, actually, it doesn’t take too much
cleverness to come up with a way to do this.

00:00:42.199 --> 00:00:48.510
Any file, whether it’s a video, or sound,
or text, code, an image, whatever, is ultimately

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:53.079
stored as a sequence of 1’s and 0’s, and
a simple strategy to correct any bit that

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:57.739
gets flipped would be to store three copies
of each bit.

00:00:57.739 --> 00:01:02.089
Then the machine reading the file could compare
these three copies and always take the best

00:01:02.089 --> 00:01:07.420
two out of three whenever there’s a discrepancy.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.030
But what that means is using two thirds of
your space for redundancy.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:15.650
And even then, for all that space given up,
there’s no strong guarantee about what happens

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.100
if more than 1 bit gets flipped.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.920
The much more interesting question is how
to make it so that errors can be corrected

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:24.710
while giving up as little space as possible.

00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:28.180
For example, using the method you’ll learn
about in this video, you could store your

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:34.770
data in 256-bit blocks, where each block uses
nine bits –Nine!– to act as a kind of

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:39.850
redundancy, the other 247 bits are free to
carry whatever meaningful message or data

00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:40.850
you want.

00:01:40.850 --> 00:01:45.250
And it will still be the case that if a bit
gets flipped, just by looking at this block

00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:50.240
and nothing more, a machine will be able to
identify that there was an error and precisely

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.780
where it was, so that it knows how to correct
it.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.520
And honestly, that feels like magic.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:59.409
And for this particular scheme, if two bits
get flipped, the machine will at least be

00:01:59.409 --> 00:02:03.080
able to detect that there were two errors,
though it won’t know how to fix them.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:08.030
We’ll talk later about how this scales for
blocks of a different size.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:11.980
Methods that let you correct errors like this
are known, reasonably enough, as “Error

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:13.599
correction code.”

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:18.189
For the better part of the last century, this
field has been a really rich source of surprisingly

00:02:18.189 --> 00:02:22.969
deep math being incorporated into devices
we all use every day.

00:02:22.969 --> 00:02:27.720
The goal here is to give you a very thorough
understanding of one of the earliest examples,

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.610
known as a Hamming code.

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:32.580
And by the way, the way I’m thinking about
the structure of this video is less about

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:37.150
explaining it as directly as possible, and
more a matter of prompting you to invent it

00:02:37.150 --> 00:02:40.200
yourself, with some a little gentle guidance
here and there.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.989
So when you feel like you see where it’s
going at some point, take that moment to pause,

00:02:43.989 --> 00:02:47.180
actively predict what the scheme is going
to be before I tell you.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:51.469
Also, if you want that understanding to get
down to the hardware level, Ben Eater has

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:55.420
made a video in conjunction with this one
showing you how to actually implement Hamming

00:02:55.420 --> 00:02:58.579
codes on breadboards, which is extremely satisfying.

00:02:58.579 --> 00:03:02.980
Now, you should know, Hamming codes are not
as widely used as more modern codes, like

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:04.769
the Reed Solomon algorithm.

00:03:04.769 --> 00:03:08.870
But there is a certain magic to the contrast
between just how impossible the task feels

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:14.200
at the start, and how utterly reasonable it
is once you learn about Hamming.

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:19.049
The basic principle of error correction is
that in a vast space of all possible messages,

00:03:19.049 --> 00:03:22.829
only some subset are going to be considered
valid messages.

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:29.200
As an analogy, think of correctly spelled
words vs incorrectly spelled words.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.299
Whenever a valid message gets altered, the
receiver is responsible for correcting what

00:03:33.299 --> 00:03:38.359
they see back to the nearest valid neighbor,
as you might do with a typo.

00:03:38.359 --> 00:03:43.120
Coming up with a concrete algorithm to efficiently
categorize messages like this, though, take

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:47.129
a certain cleverness.

00:03:47.129 --> 00:03:51.930
The story begins in the 1940s, when a young
Richard Hamming was working for Bell Labs

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:55.819
and some of his work involved using a very
big very expensive punchcard computer that

00:03:55.819 --> 00:03:57.209
he had only limited access to.

00:03:57.209 --> 00:04:01.159
And the programs he kept putting through it
kept failing because every now and then a

00:04:01.159 --> 00:04:03.109
bit would get misread.

00:04:03.109 --> 00:04:08.030
Frustration being the crucible for invention,
he got so fed up that he invented error correction

00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:09.249
codes.

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:13.159
There are many different ways to frame Hamming
codes, but as a first pass, we're going to

00:04:13.159 --> 00:04:15.840
go through it the way that Hamming himself
thought about them.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:21.530
Let’s use an example that’s simple, but
not too simple: A block of 16 bits.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:25.660
We’ll number the positions of these bits
from 0 up to 15.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:30.890
The actual data we want to store is only going
to make up 12 of these bits, while 4 of the

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.900
positions will be reserved as a kind of redundancy.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:38.080
The word “redundant” here doesn’t simply
mean copy, after all those four bits don’t

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.250
give us enough room to blindly copy the data.

00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:45.090
Instead, they'll need to be a much more nuanced
and clever kind of redundancy, not adding

00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.720
any new information, but adding resilience.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.310
You might expect these four special bits to
come nicely packaged together, maybe at the

00:04:52.310 --> 00:04:56.060
end or something like that, but as you’ll
see, having them sit in positions which are

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:59.880
powers of 2 allows for something that's really
elegant by the end.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:04.730
It also might give you a little hint about
how this scales for larger blocks.

00:05:04.730 --> 00:05:09.170
Also, technically it ends up being only 11
bits of data; you’ll find there’s a mild

00:05:09.170 --> 00:05:14.230
nuance for what goes on with position 0, but
don’t worry about that for now.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:18.440
Like any error correction algorithm, this
will involve two players: A sender who is

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:22.590
responsible for setting these four special
bits, and then a receiver who is responsible

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:26.320
for performing some checks and correcting
any errors.

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.800
Of course, the words “sender” and “receiver”
really refer to machines or software that's

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.790
doing all these checks, and the idea of a
message is meant really broadly, to include

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:35.460
things like storage.

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:40.190
After all, storing data is the thing as sending
a message, just from the past to the future

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:42.610
instead of from one place to another.

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:46.910
So that’s the setup, but before we can dive
in, we need to talk about a related idea which

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:51.160
was fresh on Hamming’s mind in the time
of his discovery, a method which lets you

00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:56.400
detect any single-bit error, but not to correct
them, known in the business as a “parity

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:57.400
check”.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:01.260
For a parity check, we separate out only a
single bit that the sender is responsible

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.910
for tuning, and the rest are free to carry
a message.

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:10.330
The only job of this special bit is to make
sure the total number of 1’s in the message

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:12.300
is an even number.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:16.890
For example, right now that total number of
1’s is 7, that's odd, so the sender needs

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:20.940
to flip that special bit to be a 1, making
the count even.

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.290
But if the block had already started off with
an even number of 1’s, then this special

00:06:25.290 --> 00:06:27.440
bit would have been kept at a 0.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:31.630
This is pretty simple, deceptively simple,
but it’s an incredibly elegant way to distill

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:37.130
the idea of change anywhere in a message to
be reflected in a single bit of information.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:43.650
Notice, if any bit of this message gets flipped,
either from 0 to 1 or 1 to 0, it changes the

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:48.100
total count of 1’s from being even to being
odd.

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:51.850
So if you're the receiver, you look at this
message, and you see an odd number of 1’s,

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:57.110
you can know, for sure, that some error occurred,
even though you might have no idea where it

00:06:57.110 --> 00:06:58.670
was.

00:06:58.670 --> 00:07:02.590
In the jargon, whether a group of bits has
an even or odd number of 1’s is known as

00:07:02.590 --> 00:07:04.920
its “parity”.

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:08.770
You could also use numbers and say that the
parity is 0 or 1, which is typically more

00:07:08.770 --> 00:07:11.290
helpful once you start doing math with the
idea.

00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:16.130
And this special bit that the sender uses
to control the parity is called a “parity

00:07:16.130 --> 00:07:17.130
bit”.

00:07:17.130 --> 00:07:22.430
And actually, we should be clear, if the receiver
sees an odd parity, it doesn't necessarily

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.700
mean there was just 1 error.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:28.420
There might have been 3 errors, or 5, or any
other odd number, but they can know for sure

00:07:28.420 --> 00:07:29.530
that it wasn’t 0.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:35.110
On the other hand, if there had been 2 errors,
or any even number of errors, that final count

00:07:35.110 --> 00:07:39.550
of 1’s would still be even, so the receiver
can’t have full confidence that an even

00:07:39.550 --> 00:07:43.360
count necessarily means the message is error-free.

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:47.170
You might complain that a method which gets
messed up by only two bit flips is pretty

00:07:47.170 --> 00:07:49.220
weak, and you would be absolutely right!

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:54.330
Keep in mind, though, there is no method for
error detection, or correction, that could

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:59.730
give you 100% confidence that the message
you receive is the one the sender intended.

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:03.750
After all, enough random noise can always
change one valid message into another valid

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.900
message just by pure chance.

00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:10.520
Instead, the goal is to come up with a scheme
that's robust up to a certain maximum number

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:16.460
of errors or maybe to reduce the probability
of a false positive like this.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:21.310
Parity checks on their own are weak, but by
distilling the idea of change across a full

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:26.730
message down to a single bit, what they give
us is a powerful building block for more sophisticated

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:27.980
schemes.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:32.800
For example, as Hamming was searching for
a way to identify where an error happened,

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:37.070
not just that it happened, his key insight
was that if you apply some parity checks,

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:42.090
not to the full message, but to certain carefully
selected subsets, you can ask a more refined

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:46.840
series of questions that pin down the location
of any single-bit error.

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:51.300
The overall feeling is a bit like playing
a game of 20 questions, asking yes or no queries

00:08:51.300 --> 00:08:54.300
that chop the space of possibilities in half.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:59.210
For example, let's say we do a parity check
on just these 8 bits, all the odd-numbered

00:08:59.210 --> 00:09:00.390
positions.

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:04.550
Then if an error is detected, it gives the
receiver a little more information about where

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:09.140
specifically the error is; namely that it’s
in an odd position.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:14.590
If no error is detected among those 8 bits,
it either means there's no error at all, or

00:09:14.590 --> 00:09:17.220
it sits somewhere in the even position.

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.540
You might think that limiting a parity check
to half the bits makes it less effective,

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:25.430
but when it’s done in conjunction with other
well-chosen parity checks, it counterintuitively

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.410
gives us something more powerful.

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:34.400
To actually set up that parity check, remember,
it requires earmarking some special bit that

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:37.560
has control for the parity of that full group.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.540
Here let's just choose position number 1.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:43.940
So for the example shown, the parity of these
8 bits is currently odd, so the sender is

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.200
responsible for toggling that parity bit,
and now it's even.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:51.000
This is only one out of four parity checks
we’ll do.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:55.060
The second check is going to be among the
8 bits on the right half of the grid, at least

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.860
as we've drawn it here.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.850
This time, we might use position number 2
as a parity bit.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:04.460
So these 8 bits already have an even parity,
and the sender can feel good leaving that

00:10:04.460 --> 00:10:06.600
bit number 2 unchanged.

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:11.000
Then, on the other end, if the receiver checks
the parity of this group and they find that

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.600
it’s odd, they’ll know that the error
is somewhere among these 8 bits on the right.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.500
Otherwise, it means either there's no error,
or the error is somewhere on the left half.

00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:24.180
Or I guess there could have been two errors,
but for right now we’re going to assume

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:28.350
that there’s at most one error in the entire
block; things break down completely for more

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:29.350
than that.

00:10:29.350 --> 00:10:32.890
Here, before looking at the next two checks,
take a moment to think about what these first

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:35.290
two allow us to do when you consider them
together.

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:40.230
Let's say you detect an error among the odd
columns, and among the right half.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:43.900
It necessarily means the error is somewhere
in the last column.

00:10:43.900 --> 00:10:48.060
If there was no error in the odd column, but
there was one in the right half, well that

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:50.140
tells you it's in the second to last column.

00:10:50.140 --> 00:10:54.700
Likewise, if there is an error in the odd
columns but not the right half, you know that

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.070
it's somewhere in the second column.

00:10:56.070 --> 00:11:00.790
And then, if neither of those two parity checks
detects anything, it means the only place

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:05.200
an error could be is in that leftmost column,
but it also might simply mean there’s no

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:06.460
error at all.

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:10.540
Which is all a rather belabored way to say
that two parity checks let us pin down the

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:11.620
column.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:13.980
From here, you can probably guess what follows.

00:11:13.980 --> 00:11:16.260
We do basically the same thing, but for the
rows.

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:21.070
There’s going to be a parity check on the
odd rows, using position 4 as a parity bit.

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:26.600
So in this example, that group already has
even parity, so bit 4 would be set to a 0.

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:31.250
And finally there's a parity check on the
bottom two rows, using position 8 as a parity

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:32.250
bit.

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:35.850
In this case, it looks like the sender needs
to turn on bit 8 on in order to give that

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:37.600
group an even parity.

00:11:37.600 --> 00:11:41.320
Just as the first two checks let us pin down
the column, these next two let you pin down

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:42.960
the row.

00:11:42.960 --> 00:11:47.920
As an example, imagine that during transmission
there’s an error at, say position 3.

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.220
Well, this affects the first parity group,
and it also affects the second parity group,

00:11:52.220 --> 00:11:56.200
so the receiver knows that there's an error
somewhere in that right column.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:12:01.350
But it doesn’t affect the third group, and
it doesn't affect the fourth group.

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:05.440
And that lets the receiver pinpoint the error
up to the first row, which necessarily means

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.620
position 3, so they can fix the error.

00:12:08.620 --> 00:12:12.600
You might enjoy taking a moment to convince
yourself that the answers to these four questions

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:16.839
really will always let you pin down a specific
location, no matter where they turn out to

00:12:16.839 --> 00:12:17.839
be.

00:12:17.839 --> 00:12:22.880
In fact, the astute among you might even notice
a connection between these questions and binary

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:23.880
counting.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:27.810
And if you do, again let me emphasize, pause,
try for yourself to draw the connection before

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:30.850
I spoil it.

00:12:30.850 --> 00:12:35.530
If you’re wondering what happens if a parity
bit itself gets affected…well you can just

00:12:35.530 --> 00:12:36.550
try it.

00:12:36.550 --> 00:12:40.700
Take a moment to think about how any error
among these four special bits is going to

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:47.430
be tracked down just like any other, with
the same game of 4 questions.

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:50.490
It doesn’t really matter, since at the end
of the day what we want is to protect the

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:54.550
message bits, the error correction bits are
just riding along, but protecting those bits

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:59.240
as well is something that naturally falls
out of this scheme as a byproduct.

00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:02.370
You might also enjoy anticipating how this
scales.

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:08.029
If we used a block of size 256 bits, for example,
in order to pin down a location, you need

00:13:08.029 --> 00:13:15.720
only 8 yes or no questions to binary search
your way down to some specific spot.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:19.700
And remember, each question requires giving
up only a single bit to set the appropriate

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:23.350
parity check.

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:26.960
Some of you may already see it, but we’ll
talk later about the systematic way to find

00:13:26.960 --> 00:13:29.770
what these questions are in just a minute
or two.

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:33.460
Hopefully, this sketch is enough to appreciate
the efficiency of what we’re developing

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:34.480
here.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.510
Everything except for those 8 highlighted
parity bits, can be whatever you want it to

00:13:38.510 --> 00:13:41.730
be, carrying whatever message or data you
want.

00:13:41.730 --> 00:13:45.270
The 8 bits are “redundant” in the sense
that they’re completely determined by the

00:13:45.270 --> 00:13:49.260
rest of the message, but it’s in a much
smarter way than simply copying the message

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:53.650
as a whole.

00:13:53.650 --> 00:13:59.070
And still, for so little given up, you would
be able to identify and fix any single bit

00:13:59.070 --> 00:14:00.070
error.

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:01.070
Well...almost.

00:14:01.070 --> 00:14:05.980
Okay, so, the one problem here is that if
none of the four parity checks detect an error,

00:14:05.980 --> 00:14:10.110
meaning that the specially selected subsets
of eights bits all have even parities just

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:15.610
like the sender intended, then it either means
there was no error at all, or it narrows us

00:14:15.610 --> 00:14:17.750
down into position 0.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:22.770
You see, with four yes or no questions, we
have 16 possible outcomes for our parity checks.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:27.910
And at first, that feels perfect for pinpointing
1 of the 16 positions in the block.

00:14:27.910 --> 00:14:33.000
But you also need to communicate a 17th outcome,
the “no error” condition.

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:37.870
The solution here is actually pretty simple:
Just forget about 0th bit entirely.

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:41.920
So when we do our four parity checks, and
we see that they're all even, it unambiguously

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:44.320
means that there is no error.

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:49.240
What that means, is that rather than working
with a 16-bit block, work with a 15-bit block,

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:54.050
where 11 of the bits are free to carry a message,
and 4 of them are there for redundancy.

00:14:54.050 --> 00:14:58.360
And with that, we now have what people in
the business would refer to as a “(15, 11)

00:14:58.360 --> 00:14:59.910
Hamming code”.

00:14:59.910 --> 00:15:04.120
That said, it is nice to have block size that's
a clean power of 2, and there’s a clever

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:08.850
way that we can keep that 0th bit around and
get it to do a little extra work for us.

00:15:08.850 --> 00:15:13.550
If we use it as a parity bit across the whole
block, it lets us actually detect, even though

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:15.670
we can't correct, 2-bit errors.

00:15:15.670 --> 00:15:17.410
Here’s how it works.

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:21.709
After setting those four special error-correcting
bits, we set that 0th bit so that the parity

00:15:21.709 --> 00:15:25.660
of the full block is even, just like a normal
parity check.

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:29.741
Now if there’s a single-bit error, then
the parity of the full block toggles to be

00:15:29.741 --> 00:15:33.970
odd, but we would catch that anyway thanks
to our four error-correcting checks.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:38.060
However, if there are two errors, then the
overall parity is going to toggle back to

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:42.620
being even, but the receiver will still see
that there’s been at least some error because

00:15:42.620 --> 00:15:45.790
of what's going on with the four usual parity
checks.

00:15:45.790 --> 00:15:50.240
So if they notice an even parity overall,
but something nonzero happening with the other

00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:52.860
checks, it tells them there were at least
two errors.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:54.410
Isn’t that clever?

00:15:54.410 --> 00:15:58.630
Even though we can’t correct those two-bit
errors, just by putting that one little bothersome

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:02.330
0th bit to work, it lets us detect them.

00:16:02.330 --> 00:16:06.810
This is pretty standard, it's known as an
“extended” hamming code.

00:16:06.810 --> 00:16:10.860
Technically speaking, you now have a full
description of what a Hamming code does, at

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:13.580
least for the example of a 16-bit block.

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:17.300
But I think you'll find it more satisfying
to check your understanding and solidify everything

00:16:17.300 --> 00:16:21.399
up to this point by doing one full example
from start to finish yourself.

00:16:21.399 --> 00:16:24.990
I’ll step through it with you, though, so
you can check yourself.

00:16:24.990 --> 00:16:29.180
To set up a message, whether that’s a literal
message that you’re transmitting over space,

00:16:29.180 --> 00:16:34.649
or some data that you want to store over time,
the first step is to divide it up into 11-bit

00:16:34.649 --> 00:16:35.680
chunks.

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:40.770
Each chunk is going to get packaged into an
error-resistant 16-bit block, so let’s take

00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:43.269
this one as an example and actually work it
out.

00:16:43.269 --> 00:16:46.740
Go ahead, actually do it, pause and try putting
together this block

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:51.070
....okay, you ready?

00:16:51.070 --> 00:16:59.210
Remember, position 0 along with all the powers
of 2 are reserved for error-correction duty,

00:16:59.210 --> 00:17:05.709
so you start by placing the message bits in
all of the remaining spots, in order.

00:17:05.709 --> 00:17:10.130
You need this group to have even parity, which
it already does, so you should have set the

00:17:10.130 --> 00:17:13.140
parity bit in position 1 to be a 0.

00:17:13.140 --> 00:17:19.280
The next group starts off with odd parity,
so you should have set its parity bit to 1.

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:23.549
The group after that starts with odd parity,
so again you should have set it’s parity

00:17:23.549 --> 00:17:24.549
bit to 1.

00:17:24.549 --> 00:17:31.500
And the final group also has odd parity, meaning
we set that bit in position 8 to be a 1.

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:36.300
And then, as the final step, the full block
now has even parity, meaning that you can

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:41.490
set that bit number 0, the overarching parity
bit, to be 0.

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:45.760
So as this block is sent off, the parity of
the four special subsets, and the block as

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:48.940
a whole, will all be even, or zero.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:53.610
As the second part of the exercise, let’s
have you play the role of the receiver.

00:17:53.610 --> 00:17:57.590
Of course, that would mean you don’t already
know what this message is; maybe some of you

00:17:57.590 --> 00:18:00.160
memorized it but, let’s assume that you
haven’t.

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:05.320
What I’m going to do is change either zero,
one or two of the bits in that block, and

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:08.120
then ask you to figure out what it is that
I did.

00:18:08.120 --> 00:18:15.620
So, again, pause and try working it out.

00:18:15.620 --> 00:18:23.880
...okay, so you as the receiver now check
the first parity group and you can see that

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:29.799
it’s even, so any error that exists would
have to be in an even column.

00:18:29.799 --> 00:18:34.450
The next check gives us an odd number, telling
us both that there’s at least one error,

00:18:34.450 --> 00:18:38.730
and narrowing us down into this specific column.

00:18:38.730 --> 00:18:41.950
The third check is even, chopping down the
possibilities even further.

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:46.780
And the last parity check is odd, telling
us there’s an error somewhere in the bottom,

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:50.490
which by now we can see must be in position
number 10.

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:56.110
What’s more, the parity of the whole block
is odd, giving us confidence that there was

00:18:56.110 --> 00:19:01.570
one flip and not two (if it’s three or more,
all bets are off).

00:19:01.570 --> 00:19:06.490
After correcting that bit number 10, pulling
out the 11 bits that were not used for correction

00:19:06.490 --> 00:19:11.410
gives us the relevant segment of the original
message, which if you rewind and compare,

00:19:11.410 --> 00:19:16.120
is indeed exactly what we started the example
with.

00:19:16.120 --> 00:19:19.230
And now that you know how to do all this by
hand, I’d like to show you how you can carry

00:19:19.230 --> 00:19:23.910
out the core part of all of this logic with
a single line of python code.

00:19:23.910 --> 00:19:28.260
You see, what I haven’t told you yet is
just how elegant this algorithm really is,

00:19:28.260 --> 00:19:32.350
how simple it is to get a machine to point
to the position of an error, how to systematically

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:37.650
scale it, and how we can frame all of this
as one single operation rather than multiple

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:39.549
separate checks.

00:19:39.549 --> 00:19:41.620
To see what I mean, come join me in part 2.

